hat they had to worry  about  [Music]  if i was a street food vendor that was  looking to get a permit in los angeles i  can go to the office and apply for one  but when they ask me what i'm selling if  i see them selling fruit they're going  to say oh great well can you show us  your public health department permit  they're going to require blueprints for  my car and thousands of dollars for the  new car and there's a lot of systems  that a street food vendor has to go  through because the california retail  food code isn't designed for them  one of the key characteristics of this  campaign is that it's led by street  vendors  when people say who's the leader of the  campaign it's kind of hard to say  because it really is a collective it's  caridad and it's merced  in  yolanda and meddling and these very  powerful vendor leaders that are working  every day on the sidewalk  they're superheroes  merced is a really thoughtful and also  passionate organizer and merced was  really critical leading up to the  legalization effort here in los angeles  [Music]  is  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  let's go  the vendors used to be all along here  and then santa monica wanted to prohibit  them from entering the pier area so they  blocked this whole area off to the  public and the vendors  here's a space they could be setting up  right here  but  they've been kicked out  i was outraged by the fact that law  enforcement would go and take the  vendors last way to make a living you'd  come down here on a weekend and there  would be street food for you  and now  you're not a vendor in sight because as  soon as a vendor appears setting up  there law enforcement sweeps down  and they're ticketed and arrested  so the only vendors you're going to see  now are a few  like the man over there who's pulling  his goods along the sand because the law  requires that you have to keep moving  i'm here simply to to help these street  vendors who have been faced with  thousands and thousands of dollars in  fines  it's interesting how we think santa  monica is this liberal beacon yet when  it comes to street vendors people who  are brown or latino who may be  immigrants then those people are not  allowed they're picking and choosing who  is accepted into this community  black and brown entrepreneurs out here  that have been hustling for a long time  and have amazing ideas on how to  activate space and make spaces much more  dynamic we need to bring them in  education  my name is maria i'm a hot dog and corn  bender i've been bending in santa monica  for eight years already  today i don't have a cart because  the polices around the city is around  que nos da la policia  if they see us with the car  they find us and they throw away our  stuff it makes me upset because in a lot  of countries including mexico there's a  lot of people dying of having nothing to  eat and they're just getting here and  throwing everything to the trash even  the fruit everything waters sodas chips  everything  one of the city officers  came and tried to take my car out  when he pulled it he threw the oil on me  so i have the the marks right here when  he burned me  all i'm trying to do is to make money to  support my family my kids so this is  where we want to be  okay  i know she's leaving  so what you see behind me is the joint  law enforcement task force that the city  of santa monica is spending almost three  million dollars  over the next year and a half so that  the street vendors can be rounded up and  taken out of santa monica  here at the beach this vital part of our  culture has been taken away i can't  imagine  ever kids coming to the beach and then  not experiencing that street vendor food  of a hot dog or a fruit cup etc it's  part of the beach experience  so basically taking the vendors like  taking the culture with just wasting  money  honestly i don't understand the problem  i mean it's not right for her to leave  just just because i don't make money  and taking away their hustle is kind of  it's kind of messed up  i think in order for us to bring street  food vendors into our society we need  policies that  recognize them as entrepreneurs  that means we need to design our permit  systems in a way that they can actually  understand  and that encourages them to  participate what folks can do to help  the street vendors is call your city  council member and say please give the  street vendors a legal and safe place to  sell stop the law enforcement harassment  [Music]  [Music]  is  very good  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you

